<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaties of Tirana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1926">1926</a>–<a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Pact of Tirana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirana">Tirana</a> were two agreements between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zog-i">Ahmet Zogu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a>. The first, signed in November 1926, was a pact of friendship and security in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> guaranteed the political status quo in Albania; the second, of November 1927, was a twenty-year defensive alliance. Together with Italian loans and control of the national bank, they made Albania an Italian dependency.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>An earlier treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirana">Tirana</a>, signed on 2 August 1920, had ended fighting between Italian troops and Albanian nationalists after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>'s independence and gave up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlore">Vlorë</a>, which it had held since 1914, while keeping the island of Saseno. Italy saw Albania as its gateway to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>. Ahmet Zogu, elected president in 1925, sought to develop the economy, secure his power and win recognition from his neighbours, and knew he could not do so without outside support.</p><p>In May 1925 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> accepted an Italian proposal to found the Albanian National Bank, which acted as the treasury though it was controlled by Italian banks, and came with a five-year loan of about 2 million pounds and mineral concessions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> weighed two courses: economic penetration until a de facto <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> was reached, or permanent occupation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The first treaty, the Treaty of Peace and Security, was signed on 27 November 1926 for five years. Its five articles treated anyone who challenged the Albanian status quo as an enemy of both states, barred either party from agreements harmful to the other and provided for conciliation in disputes; it was to be registered with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>. Albania accepted Italian officers to oversee and train its army and police, and the treaty came with payments to Zogu, who feared unrest might cost him power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, answered with military action on Albania's northern border.</p><p>The second treaty, signed on 22 November 1927, was a defensive alliance for twenty years, meant to stay secret. Under its seven articles each party was to help the other if attacked, with armed force and money, and neither could make peace alone. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> was to protect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>'s territory, and Albania placed its whole military arsenal at Italy's disposal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The alliance let <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> send two hundred and eighty officers to train the Albanian army and gave its navy access to the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlore">Vlorë</a>, and a ministry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> was set up to handle Albanian affairs. In 1937 the Italian Foreign Minister, Ciano, secured a further 60 million gold francs for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>. On 7 April 1939 Italy occupied the country and Zog fled; the crown passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-iii">Victor Emmanuel III</a> on 12 April.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaties of Tirana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Short of money, he turned to Italy: an Italian-controlled national bank and development company were founded, and the <b>Treaties of Tirana</b> of 1926 and 1927 made Albania an Italian ally.</blockquote><a href="/state/albanian-republic">Albanian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
